AI transpiler (qumo/compiler/{blockalg,block_synth,collect,resynth,policy,
policy_synth,ai_route}.mojo, rl/ (packages qrl and qumo_bridge),
models/ai_transpiler; docs/AI_TRANSPILER.md):

- The block collectors, smart replacement, synthesis-model lookup, learned
  routing and hybrid pass manager are native, hardware-neutral
  re-implementations whose algorithms follow qiskit-ibm-transpiler 0.18.0
  (Copyright IBM, Apache-2.0, https://github.com/Qiskit/qiskit-ibm-transpiler),
  used as an algorithmic reference only. No source file of that project is
  copied, and no IBM-trained model weights (Hugging Face
  qiskit/ai-transpiler_*) or code from qiskit-ibm-ai-local-transpiler are
  used, redistributed or required at runtime.
- The synthesis models in models/ai_transpiler were trained locally by QUMO on
  QUMO's own coupling graphs with qiskit-gym 0.4.1 (AI4quantum, Apache-2.0,
  https://github.com/AI4quantum/qiskit-gym) and its RL engine twisterl 0.5.1
  (Apache-2.0); the routing model was trained locally by QUMO. The model
  weights are QUMO's and are licensed under Apache-2.0 with the rest of this
  repository. qiskit-gym and twisterl are no longer needed for training:
  QUMO's own training stack replaces them (next item).
- Training stack (rl/: the independent package qrl and its QUMO glue
  qumo_bridge, Apache-2.0 like the rest of this repository): a native Mojo
  re-implementation, from written specifications, of the training algorithms
  of qiskit-gym 0.4.1 (AI4quantum, Apache-2.0: environments, metrics-based
  rewards, curriculum) and twisterl 0.5.1 (IBM, Apache-2.0: PPO with
  generalised advantage estimation, BasicPolicy architecture and symmetry
  augmentation). No source code of qiskit-gym, twisterl, torch, numpy or
  safetensors is copied or transliterated. Numerical reference data under
  rl/tests/fixtures are outputs of those packages obtained offline; they carry
  no code. None of these packages is required to train, run or test QUMO.
  qrl carries copies of a few QUMO modules (qumo/ai/json.mojo,
  qumo/sim/rng.mojo, the opcode table of qumo/ir/gates.mojo, the reader half of
  qumo/compiler/policy.mojo and the environment dynamics of
  qumo/compiler/policy_synth.mojo, the heavy-hex builder of
  qumo/compiler/target.mojo) so that it does not import QUMO; they are QUMO's
  own code under the same license and say so in their headers.
- Method references: D. Kremer et al., "Practical and efficient quantum
  circuit synthesis and transpiling with Reinforcement Learning",
  arXiv:2405.13196; A. Dubal et al., "Pauli Network Circuit Synthesis with
  Reinforcement Learning", arXiv:2503.14448; P. D. Nation et al.,
  "Benchmarking the performance of quantum computing software"
  (Benchpress), arXiv:2409.08844.

